        "text": "{\"scores\":{\"heroes\":4,\"craft\":4,\"facts\":4,\"age_fit\":5,\"continuity\":4,\"delight\":4},\"evidence\":{\"heroes\":\"Lennon's penalty-taking and soccer life drive the plot: the cuju volley through the shrinking hole, the rules vote, scoring past McCrum, and the final top-corner strike on his own Saturday field. But he is a bystander in Level 6 ('Not on my watch' is followed by nothing he does) and Level 8. The penalty shootout game he is building is mentioned once and never pays off.\",\"craft\":\"Strong architecture: a real-world backyard cold open, the glowing-ball device, one mini-challenge per level, escalation to 'his field' being deleted, and a keepsake message on the ball at home. Levels 6 and 8 have no challenge, though; coins simply drop. The ending is also duplicated: 'Saturday couldn't come soon enough.' appears again under 'The End', which blunts the last line.\",\"facts\":\"Nearly everything checks out, including the 1930 two-ball final (2–1 at half, 4–2), Gaetjens, Pickles, the Dick, Kerr Ladies and the 1921–71 ban, and Ken Aston's traffic lights. However, 'the very first penalty spot in history' is wrong: in 1891 the kick was taken from a 12-yard line, and the spot came later. McCrum's goalkeeper gloves are anachronistic. The 'Newspapers back in England thought there must have been a typo!' story is a popular legend stated unhedged.\",\"age_fit\":\"At 2,633 words it sits well inside the 2,200-3,200 range. The vocabulary and wit suit a 10-year-old ('That's the most sensible silly idea I've ever heard'). The mob stampede is mild and immediately resolved, and there is no baby talk.\",\"continuity\":\"Bit, BZZZT, 'Player One', 'Level complete', the watery pixel eyes, and coins fusing into a trophy are all used faithfully. The opening nods to the video-game adventure. Two gaps: it doesn't pick up the previous ending ('THE NEXT LEVEL IS YOURS TO BUILD'), and Bit never makes the 8-bit jokes his bible entry calls for.\",\"delight\":\"The story reads aloud well, with sound words, the fence-gutter CLANG, the confused goose, McCrum laughing in the mud, and Pickles as 'the best boy.' Some stretches are Bit-lecture runs of 'said Bit'. The repeated closing line reads as a copy-paste slip a parent would stumble over.\"},\"caps_applied\":[\"none\"],\"disqualified\":false,\"overall\":4.15,\"editor_notes\":[\"Give Lennon an active challenge in Level 6 (e.g. reading the cross that sets up Gaetjens) and Level 8. Pay off his penalty shootout game in the Full Time scene, which also answers the last story's 'yours to build' ending.\",\"Fix the penalty history: have McCrum mark a line twelve yards out, not a spot. Drop the gloves. Hedge the England 'typo' legend in-story ('people like to say…').\",\"Delete the duplicated 'Saturday couldn't come soon enough.' after 'The End', and give Bit a couple of 8-bit jokes to trim his lecture passages.\"],\"best_moment\":\"\\\"That's the most sensible silly idea I've ever heard,\\\" said Lennon.\",\"worst_moment\":\"Lennon cracked his knuckles. \\\"Not on my watch.\\\"\"}"
